(
    version: 1,
    id: "m04",
    title: "Square Dance",
    chapter: 1,
    concepts: ["Odometry", "Drift"],
    world: (id: "test_track"),
    robot: Starter("rover"),
    time_limit_s: 40.0,
    grading_seeds: [1, 2, 3],
    battery_soc: (0.7, 1.0),
    brief_md: r#"
## Drive a square and come home

Drive a square with **0.5 m sides**, turning **left** at each corner, and stop where you started:
within 30 mm of the start point. The Rover passes through (0.5, 0), (0.5, 0.5) and (0, 0.5) on the
way.

Every small error in a turn grows with the distance driven. That is *drift*, and it is why real
robots add other sensors to the wheel counts.

For the third star, call `axl_mark_pose(x, y, yaw)` with the pose your program believes it has.
AXL draws it as a ghost trail, and the end of the trail must be within 10 mm of the truth.
"#,
    predict: Some((
        question: "Suppose every turn comes out 2 degrees too small. After the whole square, how far from the start does the Rover end up?",
        unit: "mm",
        measured: "turn_error_square_mm",
    )),
    criteria: [Waypoints(points: [(0.5, 0.0), (0.5, 0.5), (0.0, 0.5)], tol_m: 0.06, end_tol_m: 0.03)],
    stars: (
        (text: "End within 10 mm of the start", criteria: [EndNearStart(tol_m: 0.01)]),
        (text: "Your believed pose (axl_mark_pose) is within 10 mm of the true pose", criteria: [EndNearStart(tol_m: 0.01), BelievedPose(tol_m: 0.01)]),
    ),
    hints: (
        "A square is four times: drive 0.5 m, turn 90 degrees left. Write the two moves as functions and call them four times.",
        "Reuse what you learned in the last two missions: the distance is the wheel angle times 0.028 m, and the turn needs each wheel to roll (wheel base / 2) times the turn angle.",
        "To track where the robot believes it is, add the distance driven along the heading after every drive: x += d * cos(yaw), y += d * sin(yaw), and add 90 degrees to yaw after every turn.",
    ),
)
